A number formatter that could not show a number.

With the parse half in place (0028) Swift Publisher's margin field committed correctly and then showed
the wrong thing: type 309, commit, and the field reads 22248. That is the point value the application's
own CCMeasurementUnitsFormatter converted to, coming back out of the model unformatted.

The binding does its half properly: CIDER_BIND APPLY NSTextField.objectValue ->
CAMainWindowController.currentMarginTop = 22248, which is 309 inches at 72 points each. What is missing
is the way back. NSNumberFormatter had stringFromNumber: and no stringForObjectValue:, and NSFormatter
answers nil there on purpose because it is abstract, so an NSCell asking its formatter to render the
model value got nil and fell back to the raw description of the object.

Apple's NSNumberFormatter implements stringForObjectValue: as stringFromNumber: for an NSNumber and nil
for anything else, which is what this adds. The application's own formatter calls super for exactly
this, through objc_msgSendSuper2, as the note above NSFormatter stringForObjectValue: already records.

Measured after: typing 2 into the top margin and committing sets the model to 1440, which is 20 inches,
and THE FIELD SHOWS 20. Before, the same round trip showed 22248.
--- a/src/NSNumberFormatter.m
+++ b/src/NSNumberFormatter.m
@@ -146,6 +146,22 @@
     return YES;
 }
 
+/*
+ * THE DISPLAY HALF, and it was missing the same way the parse half was. NSFormatter answers nil here
+ * deliberately, because it is abstract, so a number formatter with no override left every bound field
+ * showing what the CELL falls back to: the raw description of the model value. Swift Publisher's
+ * margin field committed 309 correctly and then displayed 22248, the point value its own formatter
+ * had converted to and would have converted back.
+ */
+- (NSString *)stringForObjectValue:(id)obj
+{
+    if (![obj isKindOfClass:[NSNumber class]])
+    {
+        return nil;
+    }
+    return [self stringFromNumber:(NSNumber *)obj];
+}
